This printed variant of the 2008 Options II paper was released in https://www.whatdotheyknow.com/request/maths_papers_2/response/1850333/attach/3/FOI%2020210708%201%20Data.zip?cookie_passthrough=1[FOI 20210708 1 Data]. It differs from the native paper in E4(c), where the codomain is written as $Y$ rather than $X$. The unchanged solution text is reused from the corresponding native-paper draft; E4(c) supplies a counterexample to the unrestricted variant and a valid corrected theorem. The same advertised-answer exclusion for H2 is retained. The complete statement transcription is held separately.

= A
{parent=2008-printed-variant}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Groups in Action}

= A2
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2/a}

An invertible matrix is an ordered <basis> of $K^n$. After choosing $j$ independent columns, their span has $q^j$ elements, so the next column has $q^n-q^j$ choices. Thus
$$
\boxed{|\operatorname{GL}(n,q)|=\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}(q^n-q^j)}.
$$

= b
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2/b/i}

For each flag choose an adapted <basis>: its first $n_i$ vectors span $V_i$, obtained by extending a <basis> successively through the nested subspaces and finally through $V$. For two flags, the invertible <linear transformation> taking one adapted <basis> to the other takes each member of the first flag to the corresponding member of the second. Hence **the action is transitive**.

= ii
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2/b/ii}

**Yes.** Suppose $g$ maps the first flag to the second and has <determinant> $\delta\ne0$. Relative to an adapted <basis> of the first flag, scaling its first <basis> vector by $\delta^{-1}$ and fixing all others defines a transformation $h$ preserving that flag, with <determinant> $\delta^{-1}$. Then $gh$ still maps the first flag to the second and has <determinant> one. Thus the special linear <subgroup> acts transitively too.

= iii
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/A/A2/b/iii}

The <stabilizer> of the indicated flag consists of block upper triangular matrices with diagonal block sizes $1,2,2$. The diagonal blocks can be any elements of $\operatorname{GL}(1,q)$, $\operatorname{GL}(2,q)$ and $\operatorname{GL}(2,q)$. The upper off-diagonal blocks have $2+2+4=8$ arbitrary entries. Thus the <stabilizer> order is $(q-1)|\operatorname{GL}(2,q)|^2q^8$. The <orbit-stabilizer theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{|\Omega|=\frac{|\operatorname{GL}(5,q)|}{(q-1)|\operatorname{GL}(2,q)|^2q^8}
=\frac{(q^5-1)(q^4-1)(q^3-1)}{(q-1)^2(q^2-1)}}.
$$

= B
{parent=2008-printed-variant}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Introduction to Fields}

= B2
{parent=2008-printed-variant/B}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/B/B2}

The <characteristic of a field> is the least positive <integer> $p$ with $p1_F=0$, or zero if none exists. Finiteness forces a positive characteristic, since the sequence of <integer> multiples of one must repeat. If this least <integer> factored into two smaller positive factors, their nonzero field multiples would multiply to zero, contradicting the absence of zero divisors. Hence it is prime. The field is a <finite-dimensional vector space> over its prime <subfield> $\mathbb F_p$, so choosing a <basis> gives $|F|=p^a$.

A nonzero <polynomial> of degree $n$ over a field has at most $n$ roots: each root supplies a linear factor by <polynomial> division, and induction applies to the quotient. In particular $X^n-1$ has at most $n$ roots. The elements of $F^\times$ whose orders divide $n$ are precisely those roots. The permitted finite-<group> criterion therefore proves **$F^\times$ is cyclic**.

For the final assertion take an odd <Fermat prime> $p=2^m+1$, $m\geq1$. Primality forces $m$ to be a power of two: an odd factor $s>1$ in $m=2^rs$ would factor $(2^{2^r})^s+1$ nontrivially. Since $2^m\equiv-1\pmod p$, the order of two divides $2m$ but not $m$. As $2m$ is a power of two, its only divisor not dividing $m$ is $2m$ itself. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{ord}_p(2)=2m}.
$$
It generates the full <group> exactly when $2m=2^m$, which holds for $m=1,2$ and fails for $m\geq3$. Therefore the possibilities are **$p=3,5$**, equivalently $p\leq5$ among odd Fermat primes. The oddness is implicit in referring to two as a unit; $p=2$ is not an admissible case for that claim.

= C
{parent=2008-printed-variant}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Number Theory}

= C2
{parent=2008-printed-variant/C}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/C/C2}

The $d$ distinct powers $1,a,\ldots,a^{d-1}$ all solve $X^d=1$. By the allowed root bound they are all its solutions. A <residue> $b$ of order $d$ must therefore equal $a^j$; conversely $\operatorname{ord}(a^j)=d/\gcd(d,j)$, because $d\mid jt$ characterizes the exponents $t$ with $(a^j)^t=1$. This proves the equivalence with $\gcd(d,j)=1$ and gives exactly $\varphi(d)$ elements of order $d$ whenever any such element exists.

To establish existence at the full order too, put $m=p-1$ and let $N_d$ count elements of order $d$. By <Lagrange's theorem for finite groups>, these orders divide $m$. The first result gives $N_d=0$ or $\varphi(d)$. Therefore
$$
m=\sum_{d\mid m}N_d\leq\sum_{d\mid m}\varphi(d)=m,
$$
using the standard divisor-sum identity for Euler's <function>. Equality forces every deficit to vanish, so $N_m=\boxed{\varphi(p-1)}$.

Finally let $h=g^{(p-1)/8}$, which has order eight. Its fourth power is the unique nonidentity square root of one, namely $-1$. Since $h^7=h^{-1}$,
$$
(h+h^{-1})^2=h^2+2+h^{-2}=2,
$$
because $h^{-2}=h^6=-h^2$. This proves **the stated <residue> squares to two**.

= D
{parent=2008-printed-variant}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Integration}

= D3
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/a/i}

The <monotone convergence theorem> says that if measurable functions satisfy $0\leq f_1\leq f_2\leq\cdots$ and converge pointwise to $f$, then $f$ is measurable and
$$
\boxed{\int f=\lim_n\int f_n},
$$
allowing the common value to be $+\infty$.

= ii
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/a/ii}

**No.** Take $f_n=\mathbf1_{[n,n+1]}$. Each is nonnegative and integrable, and each integral is one. At every fixed real point the functions eventually vanish, so the pointwise limit is zero and its integral is zero. Monotonicity or another convergence hypothesis is essential.

= b
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/b}

The partial sums $S_N=\sum_{k=0}^Na_k$ are measurable, nonnegative and increasing. By the <monotone convergence theorem>,
$$
\boxed{\int\sum_{k=0}^\infty a_k=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\int a_k<\infty}.
$$
A nonnegative measurable <function> with finite integral is finite almost everywhere: if it were infinite on a positive-measure set, every constant multiple of that set's indicator would be a lower bound, forcing an infinite integral. Thus the series **converges almost everywhere**.

= c
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/c/i}

Substitute $t=\log x$, with $dx=e^t\,dt$. The nonnegative integral becomes
$$
\int_0^\infty t e^{-(\alpha-1)t}\,dt=\frac1{(\alpha-1)^2},
$$
by scaling and <integration by parts>. The exponential decays because $\alpha>1$, with zero boundary term at infinity, proving integrability and
$$
\boxed{\int_1^\infty x^{-\alpha}\log x\,dx=(\alpha-1)^{-2}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D3/c/ii}

For $x>1$, the geometric series gives
$$
\frac{\log x}{x^p(x-1)}=\sum_{k=0}^\infty x^{-(p+k+1)}\log x.
$$
All terms are nonnegative, so monotone convergence permits termwise integration. Part (i), with $\alpha=p+k+1>1$, gives
$$
\boxed{\int_1^\infty\frac{\log x}{x^p(x-1)}\,dx=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac1{(p+k)^2}}.
$$
This series is finite for $p>0$, which also proves integrability of the original nonnegative <function>.

= D4
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D4/a}

On a product of sigma-finite measure spaces, <Tonelli's theorem> equates product and iterated integrals for a nonnegative measurable <function>, allowing infinite values. <Fubini's theorem> says that an absolutely integrable measurable <function> has integrable sections almost everywhere, their section integrals are integrable, and both iterated integrals equal the product integral. Absolute integrability may be checked first by applying Tonelli to the absolute value.

= b
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D4/b/i}

For fixed $n>0$, Tonelli and the allowed exponential integral give
$$
\int_0^n\int_0^\infty|\sin x|e^{-tx}\,dt\,dx=\int_0^n\frac{|\sin x|}x\,dx\leq n<\infty.
$$
The point $x=0$ is irrelevant to the integral. Fubini therefore permits reversing the signed integrals. Evaluating the elementary inner integral yields
$$
\int_0^n\frac{\sin x}x\,dx
=\int_0^\infty\frac{1-e^{-nt}(t\sin n+\cos n)}{1+t^2}\,dt.
$$
The first term has integral $\pi/2$. The absolute value of the remainder is at most
$$
\int_0^\infty e^{-nt}(t+1)\,dt=\frac1{n^2}+\frac1n\to0.
$$
Consequently **the improper integral tends to $\pi/2$**. The interchange was justified on each finite strip, without asserting absolute integrability on the infinite quadrant.

= ii
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/D/D4/b/ii}

**It is not Lebesgue integrable**, because its absolute integral diverges. On $I_k=[k\pi+\pi/6,k\pi+5\pi/6]$, one has $|\sin x|\geq1/2$ and $x\leq(k+1)\pi$. Thus
$$
\int_{I_k}\frac{|\sin x|}x\,dx\geq\frac1{3(k+1)}.
$$
These disjoint-interval lower bounds have a divergent harmonic sum. The finite improper value from part (i) is therefore conditional convergence, not Lebesgue integrability.

= E
{parent=2008-printed-variant}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Topology}

= E3
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3/a}

For a fixed centre $a$, the triangle inequality gives $|d(a,x)-d(a,y)|\leq d(x,y)$, so the distance-to-centre <function> is <continuous>. Its <preimage> of $(-\infty,r]$ is the closed ball, hence is closed. Since that <closed set> contains the corresponding open ball, it contains its closure:
$$
\boxed{\overline{B(a,r)}\subseteq\overline B(a,r)}.
$$
Here the bar on the right denotes a closed ball, while the bar on the left denotes closure.

= b
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3/b}

The condition $\max(|x-1/2|,|y|)\leq1/2$ gives the closed square
$$
\boxed{[0,1]\times[-1/2,1/2]}.
$$
Its four edges and corners are included, as shown in the left panel of the figure below.

= c
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3/c}

Intersect the ambient balls with $E$. All points on the vertical arm have distance at least $1/2$ from the centre, so the open ball is
$$
\boxed{(0,1)\times\{0\}}.
$$
The closed ball also contains the endpoints of that segment and the lower half of the vertical arm:
$$
\boxed{([0,1]\times\{0\})\cup(\{0\}\times[0,1/2])}.
$$
The closure of the open ball in $E$ is just $[0,1]\times\{0\}$. Both endpoints are limits of points in the open segment, whereas a vertical point of positive height has a small enough neighbourhood disjoint from it. Thus every point $(0,y)$ with $0<y\leq1/2$ witnesses strict containment.

\Image[2008-metric-balls.png]
{title=Ambient and relative maximum-norm balls}
{description=The square on the left is the ambient closed ball. On the right, the open ball is the horizontal segment without its endpoints; its closure omits the additional vertical part of the relative closed ball.}

= d
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E3/d}

Take radius $r>0$. Part (a) gives one containment. For a point $x$ with $\|x-a\|\leq r$, define $x_t=a+t(x-a)$ for $0\leq t<1$. Then $\|x_t-a\|=t\|x-a\|<r$, while $x_t\to x$ as $t\uparrow1$. Hence every point of the closed ball belongs to the closure of the open ball, proving **equality**. Positive radius is the standard open-ball convention; a zero-radius open ball would be empty.

= E4
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4/a}

In the usual <real line>, take $\boxed{F_n=[n,\infty)}$ for $n\geq1$. The sets are closed, nonempty and nested, but no real number belongs to all of them.

= b
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4}
{scope}

= i
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4/b/i}

Every $K_n$ is closed in the <Hausdorff> ambient space, because it is <compact>. Inside the <compact> space $K_1$, these sets have the <finite intersection property>: a finite <intersection> equals the member with largest index and is nonempty. If the total <intersection> were empty, their open complements would cover $K_1$; a finite <subcover> would contradict that property. Thus
$$
\boxed{\bigcap_nK_n\ne\varnothing}.
$$

= ii
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4/b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4/b/ii}

If no $K_n$ were contained in $U$, the sets $L_n=K_n\setminus U$ would be nonempty, nested and <compact>, since each is closed in $K_n$. Part (i) would supply a point in $\bigcap_nL_n$, belonging to every $K_n$ but not to $U$, a contradiction. Thus some $K_{n_0}\subseteq U$, and nesting gives **the same containment for every later index**.

= c
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/E/E4/c}

**The claim is false for an arbitrary codomain $Y$.** Let $X=[0,1]$ with its usual topology, let $Y$ be the <Sierpiński space> with open sets $\varnothing,\{1\},Y$, and define $f(0)=0$, $f(x)=1$ for $x>0$. This is a <continuous map>: the only nontrivial open-set preimage is $f^{-1}(\{1\})=(0,1]$, which is relatively open in $X$.

The sets $K_n=[0,1/n]$ are nonempty, nested and compact. But
$$
\boxed{f\!\left(\bigcap_nK_n\right)=\{0\}\ne\{0,1\}=\bigcap_nf(K_n).}
$$
Thus the extra generality in the printed codomain changes the assertion materially. This is the <failure of nested compact image intersection without closed fibers>.

If the codomain is a <T1 space>, the <continuous images of nested compact intersections> theorem does give the desired equality. The forward inclusion holds for every map. For the reverse, take $y\in\bigcap_nf(K_n)$ and set $F_n=K_n\cap f^{-1}(\{y\})$. The fibers are closed by continuity and the T1 property, so the $F_n$ are nested nonempty closed subsets of the compact set $K_1$. The <finite intersection property> gives $x\in\bigcap_nF_n$, with $f(x)=y$. It is enough that these particular fibers be closed in $K_1$; a Hausdorff codomain, as in the native self-map formulation, is a familiar sufficient assumption.

= F
{parent=2008-printed-variant}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Multivariable Calculus}

= F2
{parent=2008-printed-variant/F}
{scope}
{c}

= a
{parent=2008-printed-variant/F/F2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/F/F2/a}

A two-dimensional embedded submanifold is locally flattened by a continuously differentiable coordinate diffeomorphism of an ambient <open neighbourhood> onto a coordinate plane, or equivalently locally admits a regular two-parameter chart. A tangent vector at $x$ is the velocity $\gamma'(0)$ of a differentiable curve in $M$ through $x$. All such vectors form the two-dimensional <tangent space> $T_xM$, the image of the differential of a regular parametrization. Normal vectors form its <orthogonal> complement $(T_xM)^\perp$ in $\mathbb R^3$, which has **dimension one**.

= b
{parent=2008-printed-variant/F/F2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/F/F2/b}

The defining <function> $h(x)=x_1^2/a^2+x_2^2/b^2+x_3^2/c^2-1$ is smooth and has <gradient> $2(x_1/a^2,x_2/b^2,x_3/c^2)$. This cannot vanish on its zero level, since the origin does not lie there. The regular level-set theorem therefore makes $M$ a two-dimensional submanifold, with <tangent space> $\ker Dh(x)$ and normal space
$$
\boxed{(T_xM)^\perp=\operatorname{span}\{(x_1/a^2,x_2/b^2,x_3/c^2)\}}.
$$

= c
{parent=2008-printed-variant/F/F2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/F/F2/c}

The <Lagrange multiplier> condition says that at a constrained local extremum of a continuously differentiable objective on a regular surface $h=0$, its <gradient> is normal to the tangent plane: $\nabla f=\lambda\nabla h$ for some scalar multiplier.

First the box must be centred at zero. If the two face coordinates in one direction are $u_i,v_i$, subtracting the <ellipsoid> equations for two vertices differing only in that coordinate gives $u_i^2=v_i^2$. A nondegenerate box has $u_i\ne v_i$, hence $u_i=-v_i$. Its positive half-sides $x,y,z$ therefore satisfy $x^2/a^2+y^2/b^2+z^2/c^2=1$ and its volume is $8xyz$.

The feasible closed octant is <compact>, the boundary gives zero volume, and some interior box has positive volume, so the maximum is interior. Apply multipliers to $\log(xyz)$ there:
$$
1/x=2\lambda x/a^2,\quad1/y=2\lambda y/b^2,\quad1/z=2\lambda z/c^2.
$$
Thus the three squared scaled coordinates are equal and sum to one. The unique positive solution gives
$$
\boxed{\text{side lengths }2a/\sqrt3,\ 2b/\sqrt3,\ 2c/\sqrt3;\qquad V_{\max}=\frac{8abc}{3\sqrt3}}.
$$

= G
{parent=2008-printed-variant}
{scope}
{c}
{title2=Calculus of Variations}

= G2
{parent=2008-printed-variant/G}
{scope}
{c}

= Solution
{parent=2008-printed-variant/G/G2}

For the regular Sturm-Liouville setting assume $p$ is positive and continuously differentiable, $q,w$ are <continuous>, and $w>0$. Apply a multiplier to $J[y]-\lambda(\int_a^bwy^2-1)$. Its <first variation>, divided by two, is
$$
[p y'\eta]_a^b-\int_a^b[(py')'+qy+\lambda wy]\eta\,dx.
$$
Endpoint-vanishing variations and the allowed fundamental lemma give
$$
\boxed{-(py')'-qy=\lambda wy}.
$$
If an endpoint value is free, its <variation> is arbitrary, giving the natural condition $py'=0$ at that endpoint. At a prescribed endpoint value the <variation> vanishes there, so no additional natural condition is imposed.

For eigenfunctions $u,v$ with different eigenvalues, multiply their equations by $v,u$ and subtract after integration. The resulting boundary expression $[p(uv'-u'v)]_a^b$ vanishes under common homogeneous Dirichlet or common natural boundary conditions. Hence $(\lambda_u-\lambda_v)\int_a^bwuv=0$, proving weighted <orthogonality>.

For the final Dirichlet problem the normalized stationary modes are
$$
\boxed{y_k(x)=\pm\sqrt2\sin(k\pi x),\qquad\lambda_k=k^2\pi^2-1,\quad k\geq1}.
$$
Solving $y''+(\lambda+1)y=0$ with both endpoint values zero gives these and excludes nonpositive $\lambda+1$. Distinct sine modes have zero integral product on $[0,1]$. Their Dirichlet values also make the same boundary expression vanish, which explains <orthogonality> even though their endpoint derivatives do not obey the natural conditions.

For global minimality, the Fourier sine expansion of an endpoint-vanishing <function> and Parseval's identities give $\int y'^2=\sum k^2\pi^2a_k^2\geq\pi^2\sum a_k^2=\pi^2\int y^2$. Equality holds exactly in the first sine eigenspace. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\min J=\pi^2-1=\lambda_1,\qquad y=\pm\sqrt2\sin(\pi x)}.
$$
Distinct modes are <orthogonal>; the two sign choices of the same minimum are not <orthogonal> to each other. This distinguishes the family of stationary modes from the two representatives of the minimizing eigenspace in the question's wording.

= H
